    {
      "id": "E9GrnbOxQ05KNs_A4CbWl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4916.031746031743,
      "y": -1977.988095238095,
      "width": 2028.4666666666642,
      "height": 1529.9999999999989,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1013022340,
      "version": 1264,
      "versionNonce": 530849852,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "yyfv2L5AojZOWpPZGyi0G",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1788363576510,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A runtime structure that keeps track of everything JavaScript needs while executing a particular piece of code.\n\nThere are mainly 3 types :- \n\n1. Global Execution Context :- Created when your JavaScript program starts.\n2. Function Execution Context :- Created every time a function is called.\n3. Eval Execution Context :- Created when eval() executes code. You generally won't use eval(), so don't worry\nabout this one for now.\n\nHow it look like :-\n \nGlobal Execution Context\n├── x -> variable\n├── add -> function\n\nFunction Execution Context\n├── a = 5\n├── b = 3\n├── result = 8\n\nThink :- \"Where/how this code is currently executing\"\n\n\"Where\" refers to the scope and environment the code sit like when we create a variable globally i can be accessable\nby everyone but Code running inside a specific function. It has its own private workspace containing its\nparameters and locally declared variables.\n\n\"How\" refers to the rules, mechanisms, and internal setup applied to run that code. for e.g.\nHow the code determines what this points to , \nit do Hoisting means it parse the code text to find keywords like var, let, const, and function and reserve memory\nfor them :- \n     -  Thats why when you call a function before creating it. it works fines because all declarations were magically\n\"hoisted\" (pulled) to the very top of the file or function.\n     - when you call the var vraiuable before creating it. it give undefined\n     - when call let / const before creating give Throws a ReferenceError if accessed",
      "fontSize": 35.99999999999997,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A runtime structure that keeps track of everything JavaScript needs while executing a particular piece of code.\n\nThere are mainly 3 types :- \n\n1. Global Execution Context :- Created when your JavaScript program starts.\n2. Function Execution Context :- Created every time a function is called.\n3. Eval Execution Context :- Created when eval() executes code. You generally won't use eval(), so don't worry about this one for now.\n\nHow it look like :-\n \nGlobal Execution Context\n├── x -> variable\n├── add -> function\n\nFunction Execution Context\n├── a = 5\n├── b = 3\n├── result = 8\n\nThink :- \"Where/how this code is currently executing\"\n\n\"Where\" refers to the scope and environment the code sit like when we create a variable globally i can be accessable by everyone but Code running inside a specific function. It has its own private workspace containing its parameters and locally declared variables.\n\n\"How\" refers to the rules, mechanisms, and internal setup applied to run that code. for e.g.\nHow the code determines what this points to , \nit do Hoisting means it parse the code text to find keywords like var, let, const, and function and reserve memory for them :- \n     -  Thats why when you call a function before creating it. it works fines because all declarations were magically \"hoisted\" (pulled) to the very top of the file or function.\n     - when you call the var vraiuable before creating it. it give undefined\n     - when call let / const before creating give Throws a ReferenceError if accessed",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "c8snNs9dgLIuhnCmaEK06",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4924.762486772484,
      "y": 93.01190476190527,
      "width": 2051.005185185182,
      "height": 318.4999999999996,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 877818044,
      "version": 1648,
      "versionNonce": 477215548,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788363574222,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Every time a function is called, JavaScript creates a function execution context.\nWhere does JavaScript keep track of those execution contexts while they are running? That's where the Call Stack\ncomes in. ( Last In, First Out )\n\nJavaScript is generally single-threaded.\nImp :- Meaning, one JavaScript execution thread has one call stack and can execute one piece of JavaScript at a\ntime. as it runs line by line",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Every time a function is called, JavaScript creates a function execution context.\nWhere does JavaScript keep track of those execution contexts while they are running? That's where the Call Stack comes in. ( Last In, First Out )\n\nJavaScript is generally single-threaded.\nImp :- Meaning, one JavaScript execution thread has one call stack and can execute one piece of JavaScript at a time. as it runs line by line",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "uHR8Wh_-h36zGxo_30gm8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7268.462854398034,
      "y": 174.894259905504,
      "width": 2292.6718518518474,
      "height": 318.4999999999996,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5d",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 115411332,
      "version": 2235,
      "versionNonce": 269663420,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788364418342,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. What is Scope? :- Scope means: where a variable can be accessed. like globally initialize veriable can be access any where in the\nfile but when we have initialize the variable inside a function then it only available in function only \n\nJS follows Lexical scope which means A function looks for variables based on Where the function is written and here we get the\nconcept of Lexical Environment \n\nWhat is Lexical Environment? : - When JavaScript executes code, it needs somewhere to keep track of things like:",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. What is Scope? :- Scope means: where a variable can be accessed. like globally initialize veriable can be access any where in the file but when we have initialize the variable inside a function then it only available in function only \n\nJS follows Lexical scope which means A function looks for variables based on Where the function is written and here we get the concept of Lexical Environment \n\nWhat is Lexical Environment? : - When JavaScript executes code, it needs somewhere to keep track of things like:",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "uI1f9mg07047aT86WjUhl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7369.534282969464,
      "y": -848.439073427829,
      "width": 964.3385185185134,
      "height": 454.99999999999943,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5f",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 62533636,
      "version": 2932,
      "versionNonce": 1776074812,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788367760968,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Hoisting does not literally mean: \"JavaScript physically\nmoves the code to the top.\"\n\nDuring the creation/setup phase of an execution\ncontext, JavaScript creates bindings for declarations\nbefore executing the code. \nnow they get declare but get initialized when the code\nget start executing and the time between declaration\nand execution is called the TDZ (Temporal Dead\nZone)",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Hoisting does not literally mean: \"JavaScript physically moves the code to the top.\"\n\nDuring the creation/setup phase of an execution context, JavaScript creates bindings for declarations before executing the code. \nnow they get declare but get initialized when the code get start executing and the time between declaration and execution is called the TDZ (Temporal Dead Zone)",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1tjEiFOQRjdE34dVsabw3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7321.200949636131,
      "y": -1985.1057400944965,
      "width": 1507.6718518518467,
      "height": 773.4999999999991,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5fV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1403809980,
      "version": 4529,
      "versionNonce": 1499523644,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788369306447,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now lets understand this from example only \n\nnow generally when we call a function then it get it new function execution context\nthen its variable are created in its scope and also lexical environment is also created\nfor it and when the function returns then it get removed from the call-stack and\nalso its lexical environment get removed by garbage-collecter\n\nBut when we have a function in which we are returning a another function and we are\nusing the variable of a outer function in the returning function then it becomes a\nclosure now lets understand it through a e.g.\n\nnow in this we are calling outer() and store the returning function in fn when we call\nit. Its ececution context get into the stack and its lexical environment is created in\nwhich we have inner --> outer now when the outer function returns\nthe execution context get removed from the stack but lexical environment is not\nremoved by the garbage-collecter and thats why inner function is able to access the\nx from the outer even it is removed from stack.",
      "fontSize": 36.399999999999956,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now lets understand this from example only \n\nnow generally when we call a function then it get it new function execution context then its variable are created in its scope and also lexical environment is also created for it and when the function returns then it get removed from the call-stack and also its lexical environment get removed by garbage-collecter\n\nBut when we have a function in which we are returning a another function and we are using the variable of a outer function in the returning function then it becomes a closure now lets understand it through a e.g.\n\nnow in this we are calling outer() and store the returning function in fn when we call it. Its ececution context get into the stack and its lexical environment is created in which we have inner --> outer now when the outer function returns\nthe execution context get removed from the stack but lexical environment is not removed by the garbage-collecter and thats why inner function is able to access the x from the outer even it is removed from stack.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
